pub const LESSON_GOALS : [27]str = [
    "What a clock domain is, the four ways clock_domain.t27 names to cross one, and what the native compiler checks of them.",
    "What a clock carries besides frequency, what jitter does to an edge, and what a tool-card spec is for.",
    "Where a board's clock really comes from, and how a ring oscillator inside the chip was measured on three dice over JTAG.",
    "Why the clock does not reach every flop at once, and what the tree delays of a 7-series chip cost.",
    "What a global buffer is, why it sits on its own network, and what one real build of the whole flow looked like.",
    "Why gating a clock with logic makes a new domain, and what a clock enable is for.",
    "How the MMCM turns one clock into another by multiply and divide, and which windows the numbers must land in.",
    "How fine the phase knob is, how the lock model behaves, and what one widened window breaks.",
    "When the analyzer checks a path between two clocks and when it must be told they are strangers.",
    "The three reset kinds, which one races the clock, and the shape the course recommends.",
    "How a reset release walks a shift register, and what the compiler emits for it.",
    "Why a reset grows a tree like a clock, and where its cost shows up in a real resource count.",
    "What setup and hold mean, what a flop does when the window is missed, and what a VCD shows of it.",
    "How MTBF prices a crossing in integer log2 arithmetic, and which constants are assumptions.",
    "Why two flops are the fix, and what the generated model of the crossing looks like.",
    "Why sampling a binary bus mid-change tears it, and what the FIFO spec checks of its pointers.",
    "Why Gray code changes one bit at a time, and what a test suite that never reads pointers cannot see.",
    "How a handshake moves a pulse between worlds, and what an edge-driven report and a toggle synchronizer share.",
    "How the flags are derived from the pointers, which signals actually cross, and what one flipped assignment breaks.",
    "How depth follows from the write rate, the read rate and the worst burst, and what a real timed loop measures.",
    "What a testbench discipline looks like when it is real, and why this lesson opens the FIFO spec and a mutation recording instead.",
    "What the create_clock line tells the analyzer, and which spec writes it.",
    "What a false path declares, what a max delay bounds instead, and what removing a check looks like in a repository.",
    "How IO timing is stated against a clock another chip drives, and what margins read from an SDF look like.",
    "What a CDC report lists and asks of every crossing, and what a report of verified things has in common with it.",
    "How one crossing was captured at the flip-flops on a real board, and what the recorded run printed.",
    "How the pieces of this course meet in one build, and what a bitstream diff says about a change."
];
pub const LESSON_TEXTS : [27]str = [
    "A clock domain is every flip-flop that shares one clock edge. Move a signal between two domains and the receiving edge can sample it mid-change: that is the whole subject of this course. clock_domain.t27 names the four ways across in one enum: no_cross, two_flop, fifo_async, handshake. Module 5 takes two_flop apart, module 6 the handshake, module 7 the FIFO. The recording runs the native t27c on this spec: 12 tests pass and 5 invariants are proved while it compiles. The player compiles the spec in your browser; where the browser cannot run a check it skips it and says why.",
    "A clock is a period with an edge in it, and the edge is not perfectly placed: cycle to cycle it wanders by the jitter. The spec of the tri clocks tool card is not a test suite, and the recording shows its native report saying exactly that: 0 tests, because it is constants a tool page reads. Every constant on that card names the tool output it came from. Jitter eats the margin a setup calculation leaves: the clock-tree spec of the next module carries 50 ps of PLL jitter as one of its constants.",
    "The clock enters the board, not the chip: an oscillator on the PCB drives a pin. The board spec of the QMTech Wukong (XC7A200T in an FGG676 package, part xc7a200tfgg676) also measures a clock that never leaves the chip: CFGMCLK, a ring oscillator with no crystal behind it. UG470 gives 65 MHz nominal over a 50-80 MHz envelope. It was measured on the three attached dice by timing a 2^24 prescaler over JTAG: 70770, 68490 and 67200 kHz. The recording runs the native t27c on the board spec: 15 tests pass, 11 invariants are proved comptime.",
    "The clock does not reach every flop at once: it arrives through a tree, and the spread of arrival times is skew. cts.t27 models that tree with the teaching delays of an Artix-7 in its header: a BUFH at 0.05 ns, a BUFG at 0.1 ns, PLL jitter of 50 ps, and a maximum skew target of 100 ps. The recording runs the native t27c on it: 15 tests pass, 2 invariants are proved comptime. Skew is not noise to remove but a budget to spend: a well-balanced tree spends it evenly, which is what makes one shared edge possible at all.",
    "A global buffer, BUFG on a 7-series chip, drives the clock on a dedicated low-skew network so the tree of the last lesson has a clean root. The widget shows the flow a BUFG sits inside: one real XC7A200T build, 116.9 s with openXC7 and 83.5 s through the t27 L3+L4 path, byte-identical bitstreams, and place and route taking 60 percent of it. The buffer is one layer of that picture, and the spec frame opens cts.t27 again, where bufg() is built and bufg_has_higher_fanout() is tested.",
    "Gating a clock with logic -- an AND gate on the clock line to save power -- cuts the clock with a signal that changes at data speed, makes glitches, and quietly creates a new clock domain of everything downstream. The rule: never gate a clock; use a clock enable, which the flop samples at the edge instead of the edge sampling it. The widget shows the discipline of proving such claims: two bitstreams that differ in three bits, and a knockout build per bit, each clearing one, so no bit can claim credit it does not earn. The spec frame opens clock_domain.t27, whose gated_clk source kind is the model of this trap.",
    "The MMCM turns one clock into another: the input is divided by D, multiplied by M into an internal VCO, and the VCO is divided by O for the output. mmcm.t27, written for this course, keeps M in eighths so fractional steps stay integer: M 2.000 to 64.000, D 1 to 106, O 1 to 128, and the VCO must land in its window -- 800 to 1600 MHz here, a teaching assumption the header labels, next to the ranges that come from UG472. From 200 MHz in, M 5, D 1: a 1000 MHz VCO, and O 10 gives 100 MHz out. The recording runs the native t27c: 16 tests pass, 4 invariants comptime.",
    "Phase moves in steps of the VCO period divided by 56 -- a faster VCO is a finer knob, which is why phase work wants the VCO high. Lock is modelled with a settling window the header labels as an assumption. The recording widens that assumption by one sed: VCO_MIN_KHZ from 800000 to 400000. The 500 MHz configuration the rejection test names slides inside the widened window, and exactly one test fails, vco_below_the_window_is_rejected -- a test that names the number it refuses is a test that guards the assumption. The recording puts the line back and prints the sha256 of the restored spec.",
    "Two clocks are related when one is made from the other -- through the MMCM of module 3 -- and then every path between them is timed: the analyzer knows they share an origin. Strangers get no such path check unless you declare one. timing.t27 is the arithmetic of that check: timing arcs, slack and Fmax with an Artix-7 teaching model, LUT 0.1 ns, BRAM 2.0 ns, DSP 2.5 ns, routing 0.3 ns. The recording runs the native t27c: 25 tests pass, 2 invariants comptime. Related clocks were module 3; making strangers safe is modules 5 to 7.",
    "A board asserts reset when it wants to, not when your clock says so: the assert may be asynchronous. The release may not be. reset_sync.t27, written for this course, names the three kinds: sync (0), where assert and release both go through the clock; async assert, sync release (1), the usual choice; and async assert, async release (2), which races recovery and removal at every flop it feeds. is_recommended() accepts kinds 0 and 1 with at least 2 stages. The recording runs the native t27c: 16 tests pass, 3 invariants comptime.",
    "The release walks a shift register: at the destination clock, flop after flop takes its neighbour's value, and the reset is held until the pipe runs dry. The latency is the stage count -- 2 stages release in 2 clocks, 3 in 3. The recording shows what the compiler emits for the model: t27c gen prints the Zig, and the step function is a struct copy, one field per flop, no more. The wc -l at the end of the recording is the size of the whole generated model.",
    "A reset reaches every flop in the design, so it grows a tree like a clock tree and is balanced for the same reason: an uneven release is a skew you built yourself. The widget counts a real routed design against the XC7A200T's own totals, read from the chipdb and prjxray-db, and counts yours if you drop your routed JSON on it. A reset tree lives in the same budget: routing and control-set pressure, visible in exactly those bars. The spec frame opens cts.t27 once more, the tree arithmetic that both trees share.",
    "Every flop has a window around its sampling edge -- setup before it, hold after it. Land a transition inside the window and the flop does not cleanly pick a level: its output can hang between 0 and 1, a metastable state that resolves at a time the datasheet does not promise. The widget reads a .vcd from any simulator and reports value changes, the busiest net, and signals stuck at X or Z: X in a simulation is the testbench's way of showing the window was missed. The spec frame opens clock_domain.t27, whose crossing model is what module 5 turns into arithmetic.",
    "MTBF prices a crossing: e^(T/tau) divided by F_clk, F_data and the window W -- the form Xilinx WP323 teaches. mtbf.t27, written for this course, computes it in integer log2 with 10 fractional bits, because the honest answer overflows every integer seconds count. tau 50 ps and W 10 ps are labelled assumptions: the vendors publish flop parameters only partially. At 100 MHz receiving a 10 MHz stream, 1 stage leaves -200 ps of slack and a Q10 log2-MTBF of -19513 -- under two microseconds; 2 stages leave 9800 ps and 275887 -- beyond any clock the universe will run. The recording runs the native t27c: 15 tests pass, 4 invariants comptime.",
    "The fix is two flops in the destination clock, nothing between them: the opening one may go metastable, and the second samples its settled output a full period later. Each extra stage multiplies MTBF by 2 to the power T_clk times log2 e over tau -- 295400 in Q10 at 100 MHz, the spec's stage_gain_q10(). The recording shows t27c gen on clock_domain.t27: the printed Zig is the crossing model the whole course leans on, and wc -l counts it. What the two flops cost is latency: two destination clocks from edge to settled output, the same price the release pipe of module 4 pays.",
    "Cross a multi-bit bus by sampling it in another domain and it tears: some bits arrive from before the change, some from after, and the value you read never existed. A binary counter is the worst carrier -- 7 to 8 flips four bits at once. fifo.t27 is the buffer that does this properly, and its head advances as (head_ptr + 1) % depth, with MAX_FIFO_DEPTH 65536. The recording runs the native t27c on it: 18 tests pass, 6 invariants comptime. What the tests check is fill counts and flags; the next lesson shows what they never check.",
    "Gray code changes exactly one bit per step, so a sample taken mid-change reads one of two adjacent codes -- off by one, never garbage. A FIFO whose read and write pointers count in Gray can be sampled across domains safely, and the flags derived from them hold. The recording plants a mutation that Gray would have caught red-handed if the tests had looked: (state.head_ptr + 1) becomes + 3, and every test still passes, because the suite asserts fill and flags, never pointer values. A test suite defends what it reads; the encoding is defended by the next lesson's rule, not by these tests. The recording restores the spec and prints its sha256.",
    "A pulse is the hardest thing to move: one clock wide here is not one clock wide there. The handshake turns it into a level: the sending side raises a request, the receiving side samples it, raises an acknowledge, and the request drops -- four edges, and the pulse became a conversation. The widget is a report that works the same way: 25 live checks, printing only rows that are NEW red or CLEARED since the last look -- the far side sees the change, not the level, exactly what a toggle synchronizer arranges in hardware. The spec frame opens clock_domain.t27, whose handshake strategy is this conversation as one enum value.",
    "The data does not cross; it lives in BRAM and both sides address it. What crosses is the pointers, in Gray, and the flags derived from them: empty when the pointers match, full when they differ by the depth. The recording breaks the flag arithmetic with one flipped assignment -- push sets flags.empty to true -- and exactly one test fails, push_increments_fill, the one that asks a push for a non-empty FIFO. The recording restores the spec and prints its sha256. The spec frame opens fifo.t27, where push and pop build the flags the crossing carries.",
    "Depth is not a feeling: writes at rate W, reads at rate R, and the buffer must hold the worst burst times (W - R) over the time it lasts. Oversize it and it costs BRAM you could have spent; undersize it and it overflows silently, because a full FIFO drops or blocks and neither shows up in a waveform. The widget is a real loop with a measured time -- 19.06 s for router1, bitwalk, xc7frames2bit and the SRAM load, on a laptop at load average about 110 -- the same arithmetic of rate and time that sizes a buffer, measured on a run that really happened. The spec frame opens fifo.t27, whose fill_count is the number this lesson is about.",
    "A testbench for the asynchronous FIFO would drive both clocks, tear the pointers on purpose and check that the flags never lie. The testbench spec in this tree is not that yet: it does not compile in the browser compiler and native runs are blocked on it, so this lesson opens fifo.t27 itself and, in place of a FIFO testbench, a recording of the discipline it would follow: 5 planted bugs in packets.t27 -- a CRC polynomial bit, a CRC address width, LFRM no-ops, a COR0 bit, a type-2 word count -- each mutant rebuilt and re-swept, every one caught. When a real testbench spec lands, this lesson takes it.",
    "Until a create_clock line exists, the analyzer sees no clock at all and times nothing: every path is unconstrained and every report is empty. The line names the port, the period and the waveform edges. emitter_xdc.t27 is the spec of the emitter that writes such lines from a pins spec -- its text carries create_clock -add -name among the lines it emits. The native test-report on this spec is blocked in the current tree, so the recording shows what does run: t27c parse of the spec, clean, and the grep that finds its create_clock lines. The player compiles the spec in your browser.",
    "set_false_path tells the analyzer a crossing is asynchronous: stop reporting it, the synchronizer owns it. Used beyond that, it silences real failures, so a CDC flow pairs it with set_max_delay, which bounds a synchronizer path instead of ignoring it -- the two or three flops must still be close in time. A false path is a check deliberately removed, and the widget shows what removing checks looks like in a repository: 14 days of merged pull requests, which added tests, changed them or removed asserts, with the exact lines from git. The spec frame opens timing.t27, the analysis the exceptions carve holes in.",
    "A pin's timing is stated against a clock another chip drives: set_input_delay and set_output_delay, negative before the edge, positive after. The widget shows what such margins look like when they are read instead of assumed: two recorded model runs over the routed E3 design for the AX7203 board (XC7A200T in an FBG484 package), printed -- smallest TX hold 3445 ps on eth_txd[1], smallest RX mid-nibble margin 10900 ps on rxctl_n, both PASS -- a model over nextpnr's own SDF of the routed design, not a board measurement, and it says so. The spec frame opens emitter_xdc.t27 again, the emitter these lines would go into.",
    "A CDC report walks every crossing in the netlist and asks each one: synchronized, and how well? It names the unsynchronized paths, the synchronizers with too few stages, the buses that forgot their Gray. The widget is another report of named things, each checked: 129 files held intact against the newest of 54 content-addressed snapshots -- not a CDC report, but the same shape, a list where every line is a verdict with a source. The spec frame opens timing.t27, the arithmetic such a report leans on when it estimates how safe a synchronizer is.",
    "One crossing, captured where it lands: at the RX flip-flops themselves. The tri fpga-rxcap command models the E3 RX capture on the AX7203 board (XC7A200T in an FBG484 package) in the same SDF the last module used, and its recorded run printed: falling-edge hold 10900 ps on rxctl_n, with RXC 2.4 ns behind its data through the BUFG, so rxd_p1 and an RX_ER nibble sample the new value over a window the model states. The spec is a tool card -- constants for a tool page, 0 tests, and the recording shows the native report saying exactly that. The pins it captures are the far end of every lesson before this one.",
    "Close the loop: change one thing about a crossing -- a synchronizer stage, a Gray pointer, a constraint -- rebuild, and ask the bitstream what moved. The widget compares two Xilinx 7-series bitstreams in your browser: header, packets, and every 101-word frame, with the differing frame address decoded; nothing is uploaded. A CDC change that claims to change nothing should diff to nothing, and one that means something shows exactly which frames it touched. The spec frame opens fifo.t27, the buffer this course built crossing by crossing, and its 18 passing tests are the receipt."
];
pub const LESSON_TASKS : [27]str = [
    "In the recording, count the passing tests and the proved invariants; then in the spec frame find the four crossing strategies and name the module of this course that takes each one apart.",
    "In the recording, find the line that says why the report counts 0 tests; then in the spec frame read the constants and find where each names the tool output it came from.",
    "In the recording, find the three measured CFGMCLK values and the busdev numbers of their dice; then in the spec frame find the UG470 envelope they are compared with and the prescaler that made them readable.",
    "In the recording, find the two buffer delays in the header; then in the spec frame find the maximum skew target and the test that compares buffer fanout.",
    "In the widget, find the two build times and what percentage place and route took; then in the spec frame find bufg() and the test that gives it the higher fanout.",
    "In the widget, find how many bits the A/B bitstreams differ by and how each was cleared; then in the spec frame find the gated-clock source kind and the strategy enum value a gated clock forces.",
    "In the recording, find the 500 MHz configuration the rejection test names; then in the spec frame find which ranges the header says come from UG472 and which are labelled assumptions.",
    "In the recording, find the sed line, the test that fails and the sha256 of the restored spec; then in the spec frame compute the phase step for a 1000 MHz VCO.",
    "In the recording, count the passing tests; then in the spec frame find the four teaching delays of the header and the function that turns arcs into slack.",
    "In the recording, find the test that calls async release racy; then in the spec frame read is_recommended() and check what it requires of kind and stages.",
    "In the recording, find the wc -l count; then in the spec frame find after_clocks() and check that release latency equals the stage count.",
    "In the widget, read the totals the bars are counted against; then in the spec frame find the skew target a reset tree is balanced to meet.",
    "In the widget, drop a .vcd if you have one and find the busiest net; then in the spec frame find the crossing model the window belongs to.",
    "In the recording, find the grep that lists the assumptions; then in the spec frame compute what stage_gain_q10() says one more flop buys at 100 MHz.",
    "In the recording, find the wc -l count of the generated model; then in the spec frame find the two_flop strategy and say which module took it apart.",
    "In the recording, count the passing tests and the proved invariants; then in the spec frame find the head pointer update and the maximum depth constant.",
    "In the recording, find the sed line and confirm that no test failed; then in the spec frame find the fill and flag assertions and point at the hole the mutation slipped through.",
    "In the widget, find how many checks the report runs and what it prints when nothing changed; then in the spec frame find the handshake strategy and count the edges its conversation takes.",
    "In the recording, find the flipped assignment and the one test that fails; then in the spec frame find where push builds the flags and derive empty from the pointers yourself.",
    "In the widget, find the loop's measured time and the load average it ran at; then in the spec frame find fill_count and write the depth formula for your own worst burst.",
    "In the recording, list the 5 planted bugs and count how many were caught; then in the spec frame find the flags a real testbench would have to tear on purpose.",
    "In the recording, find the create_clock lines the grep returns; then in the spec frame find the emitter's line builder and say what a missing create_clock does to a timing report.",
    "In the widget, find a pull request that removed asserts and the exact lines it took; then in the spec frame find the slack arithmetic a false path silences.",
    "In the widget, find the smallest TX hold and the pin it was measured on; then in the spec frame find where the emitter would put the input and output delays.",
    "In the widget, find how many files the report holds intact and how many snapshots it checks against; then in the spec frame find the estimate a CDC report would run per crossing.",
    "In the recording, find the recorded hold margin and the delay through the BUFG; then in the spec frame read the tool card's data sources and say what the model did not measure.",
    "In the widget, drop two bitstreams if you have them and find the differing frame; then in the spec frame pick one test of the FIFO and say what change would break it."
];

; --- Claims --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
test the_course_is_three_cubed {
    assert LESSONS_PER_MODULE == 3;
    assert MODULE_COUNT == 9;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 27;
    assert MODULE_COUNT * LESSONS_PER_MODULE == LESSON_COUNT;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 3 * 3 * 3;
}

test it_starts_with_one_edge_and_ends_with_a_diff {
    assert MODULE_IDS[0] == "what-a-clock-is";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[0] == "what-a-clock-is";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[0] == "t27c-clock-domain";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[0] == "specs/fpga/clock_domain.t27";
    assert MODULE_IDS[8] == "on-the-board";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[26] == "on-the-board";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[26] == "tri-blog-list";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[26] == "specs/fpga/fifo.t27";
}

test the_mmcm_module_carries_the_new_spec {
    assert MODULE_IDS[2] == "pll-and-mmcm";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[6] == "specs/fpga/mmcm.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[7] == "specs/fpga/mmcm.t27";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[6] == "t27c-mmcm";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[7] == "t27c-mmcm-mutate";
}

test the_reset_module_teaches_the_three_kinds {
    assert MODULE_IDS[3] == "resets";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[9] == "specs/fpga/reset_sync.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[10] == "specs/fpga/reset_sync.t27";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[9] == "t27c-reset-sync";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[10] == "t27c-reset-sync-gen";
}

test metastability_comes_before_the_fifo {
    assert MODULE_IDS[4] == "metastability";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[13] == "specs/fpga/mtbf.t27";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[13] == "t27c-mtbf";
    assert MODULE_IDS[6] == "async-fifo";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[16] == "t27c-fifo-mutate";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[19] == "t27-tri-competitors";
}

test the_constraints_module_names_the_emitter_twice {
    assert MODULE_IDS[7] == "constraints";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[21] == "specs/pins/emitter_xdc.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[23] == "specs/pins/emitter_xdc.t27";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[23] == "tri-fpga-rgmii";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[22] == "specs/fpga/timing.t27";
}

test the_board_module_reads_real_runs {
    assert MODULE_IDS[8] == "on-the-board";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[24] == "tri-game-vault";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[25] == "t27c-fpga-rxcap";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[26] == "tri-blog-list";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[25] == "specs/tools/trios/tri/fpga-rxcap.t27";
}

test the_course_sends_nothing {
    assert SENDS_NOTHING == true;
    assert LOCALES[0] == "en";
    assert LOCALES[1] == "ru";
}
